                            (a–l)). Their performance is then assessed using metrics such as Mean Squared Error (MSE) and the R-squared (R 2 ) score, which help determine the model's accuracy and explanatory power.

In the exploration of digital twinning and data-driven modeling, a specific type of neural network known as the Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) is adapted. The utilization of MLPs is integral to this research as they enable capturing complex relationships within the data and making accurate predictions. The core architecture of an MLP is characterized by its feedforward function. In this model data flows in a unidirectional manner, moving from the input layer to the output layer, without any recurrent connections or feedback loops. Each layer is fully connected to the subsequent layer, and the information propagates through the network, undergoing transformations and feature extraction along the way. The implementation of MLPs is realized through TensorFlow's Keras API. A sequential model is constructed by stacking dense layers, also referred to as fully connected layers. These dense layers are essential components of an MLP, and they contribute to the network's ability to learn intricate patterns in the data. Further building an effective MLP model requires careful tuning of hyperparameters. These hyperparameters include the number of hidden layers, the number of neurons in each hidden layer, and the choice of activation function for each layer. The selection of appropriate hyperparameters significantly influences the model's performance and its ability to capture the nuances within the data. To find the optimal digital twin model, an extensive hyperparameter search is conducted (see  Table 6 
                            ). A total of 3300 trials were executed, each involving different combinations of hyperparameters. This exhaustive search aimed to identify the best model (see  Fig. 10 (a–d)) configuration based on its performance metrics, specifically the R-squared (R2) and Mean Squared Error (MSE) values (see  Fig. 9 

